# Cloudflare Pages cache policy. Read by the platform at deploy time and never
# served to a visitor, which is why the bundle copies it without it being a page
# file.
#
# The page is several files where it was one, so a cache holding a stale app.js
# against a fresh index.html breaks it in a way the single-file page could not
# reach. Everything here follows from that: the shell revalidates, and a longer
# TTL is granted only where a stale copy cannot break the page AND the content
# genuinely does not change under a stable name.
#
# THE RULE THAT GOVERNS THIS FILE: matching rules are MERGED, not overridden. A
# request matching several patterns "will inherit all rules' headers", and a
# header set twice has "the values joined with a comma separator". So a broad
# `/*` rule plus a narrow one does NOT let the narrow one win — it produces one
# Cache-Control carrying both, and a cache may take the first value or the most
# restrictive, either of which discards the longer TTL entirely.
#
# Therefore every pattern below is mutually exclusive, no path matches two
# rules, and there is deliberately NO `/*` rule. Everything unlisted takes the
# platform default, which is already `public, max-age=0, must-revalidate` —
# exactly what the shell wants. index.html, app.js, lib.js, app.css, the three
# walkthrough page files, manifest.json, cities.json and the icons all rely on
# that, and adding a `/*` rule to restate it would break both rules below.
#
# config.js relies on it too, and it is the one worth naming: config.js is where
# a deploy writes the Mapbox token. Give it a long TTL and a rotated token
# outlives its replacement, leaving the page authenticating with a revoked key.
#
# This file covers the Pages origin ONLY. The PMTiles archives are served from
# R2, where a `_headers` has no effect; their Cache-Control is set at upload.
#
# Cloudflare ignores a malformed rule SILENTLY. A test asserts what this file
# says; only the cache-hit ratio on a real deploy shows that it took effect.

# Vendored libraries, fonts and basemap sprites: 16 MB that changes about twice
# a year, and the one place where re-fetching on every visit is the larger cost.
#
# THE RULE THAT MAKES A YEAR SAFE: these filenames are NOT content-hashed
# (`maplibre-gl.js`, not `maplibre-gl.a1b2c3.js`), nothing here cache-busts
# them, and hashing would need a build step, which the deploy path refuses. An
# upgrade written over the same name is therefore invisible to every warm cache
# until the year runs out. New content ⇒ new filename ⇒ update the page that
# references it. That is the rule this project already applies to published
# archives, which are never overwritten in place; vendor now follows it too.
/vendor/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# Walkthrough plates. NOT immutable, and the reason is measured rather than
# cautious: these have been re-rendered under their existing filenames five
# times, and one of them is the README's hero image. A day is long enough to
# serve a reader stepping through twenty-one panels and short enough that a
# re-render reaches everyone by tomorrow.
#
# The three walkthrough PAGE files are `walkthrough.html/.css/.js` at the root
# and do not match this pattern, which needs the literal slash. That is
# intended: they are shell, and shell revalidates.
/walkthrough/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400, must-revalidate
